1)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+9\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-2X] & =4\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "142fd15b-c875-518a-9165-bb478aa0e810" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435374 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a7a743c-110e-5e17-9b8e-c0a4ed4bf822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369653} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bc9a45-3558-56aa-b44c-19b91bed820a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369654} {:answer/id #uuid "6840aa01-0203-5583-a1fb-3ee0ba2fced3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369655} {:answer/id #uuid "53b84c0f-bed5-541b-8d3e-723a5410d69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369656} {:answer/id #uuid "288b9421-5232-599d-975c-4cacf3b01709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369655} :answer-field/id #uuid "e907f6da-cd04-5106-a3c9-cf468f523d2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aeeea4a5-1443-5d67-a950-8990e0faa656" :question/math-academy-id "q-129386" :question/problem "If $\\text{Var}[X]=3.6,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\frac{10}{3}X].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{10}{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{10}{3}X] & ={(\\frac{10}{3})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{9}\\cdot 3.6 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "98fbfae8-3ff5-5189-8ae4-c342310ec549"}] :activity/title "Properties of Variance for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435364}]}
